---
const SPECS = [
  {
    caption: "the obvious one",
    flag: '--fields "title, price:float, in_stock:bool"',
  },
  {
    caption: "and this one",
    flag: '--fields "reactor_id, coolant_temp_c:float"',
  },
];
---

<section class="section" id="fields">
  <div class="shell">
    <h2 class="h-section" data-reveal>There is no list of supported fields.</h2>

    <p class="copy mt-6" data-reveal>
      Field names are arbitrary. Both of these take <strong
        >exactly the same code path</strong
      >. No lookup table, no per-field heuristic, no vocabulary the tool has to
      be taught.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-10 grid gap-6 md:grid-cols-2">
      {
        SPECS.map((s) => (
          <figure
            class="m-0 min-w-0 rounded-card border border-rule bg-base-2 px-4.5 py-4"
            data-reveal
          >
            <figcaption class="mb-2.5 font-mono text-micro text-fg-faint">
              {s.caption}
            </figcaption>
            <code class="block min-w-0 overflow-x-auto whitespace-nowrap font-mono text-code text-fg">
              {s.flag}
            </code>
          </figure>
        ))
      }
    </div>

    <div class="mt-10 border-l-2 border-rule pl-6" data-reveal>
      <p class="copy">
        The inline type is not decoration. It is half of how a stale selector
        gets caught: <code class="font-mono text-[0.92em] text-fg"
          >price:float</code
        > returning <code class="font-mono text-[0.92em] text-fg"
          >"Add to basket"</code
        > is detectable, where a bare string is not.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
